# 1. Variables and Constants

Variables are used to **store data values** in JavaScript.

Modern JavaScript provides three ways to declare variables:

```javascript
var
let
const
```

### 🔹 `let`

Use `let` when the value may change later.

```javascript
let age = 20;

age = 21;

console.log(age);
```

Output:

```text
21
```

### 🔹 `const`

Use `const` when the variable should not be reassigned.

```javascript
const name = "Abhinav";

console.log(name);
```

You cannot reassign it:

```javascript
const age = 20;

age = 21; // ❌ Error
```

### 🔹 `var`

`var` is the older way of declaring variables.

```javascript
var city = "Kottayam";

console.log(city);
```

In modern JavaScript, **`let` and `const` are generally preferred over `var`** because they provide block scope and more predictable behavior.

### ⭐ Quick Comparison

| Keyword | Reassign | Scope | Modern Usage |
|---|---|---|---|
| `var` | ✅ Yes | Function | ⚠️ Avoid when possible |
| `let` | ✅ Yes | Block | ✅ Use when value changes |
| `const` | ❌ No | Block | ✅ Default choice |

### 💡 Best Practice

```javascript
const username = "Abhinav";
let score = 100;

score = 150;
```

> ✅ Prefer `const` by default. Use `let` only when the value needs to change.

