# 3. Primitive and Reference Types

JavaScript values can broadly be understood as **primitive values** and **reference values**.

---

## 🔹 Primitive Types

Primitive values represent a single simple value.

JavaScript has these primitive types:

```text
String
Number
BigInt
Boolean
Undefined
Null
Symbol
```

### Examples

```javascript
const name = "Abhinav";       // String
const age = 20;               // Number
const isStudent = true;       // Boolean
const value = undefined;      // Undefined
const data = null;            // Null
const bigNumber = 123n;       // BigInt
const id = Symbol("id");      // Symbol
```

You can check a value's type using:

```javascript
typeof value
```

Example:

```javascript
const age = 20;

console.log(typeof age);
```

Output:

```text
number
```

---

## 🔹 Reference Types

Objects, arrays, and functions are reference values.

### Object

```javascript
const user = {
    name: "Abhinav",
    age: 20
};
```

### Array

```javascript
const skills = ["JavaScript", "React", "Django"];
```

### Function

```javascript
function greet() {
    console.log("Hello");
}
```

---

## 🧠 Primitive vs Reference

### Primitive

```javascript
let a = 10;
let b = a;

b = 20;

console.log(a);
console.log(b);
```

Output:

```text
10
20
```

The value of `a` remains unchanged.

### Reference

```javascript
const user1 = {
    name: "Abhinav"
};

const user2 = user1;

user2.name = "Developer";

console.log(user1.name);
```

Output:

```text
Developer
```

Both variables refer to the **same object**.

### ⭐ Quick Comparison

| Primitive | Reference |
|---|---|
| String | Object |
| Number | Array |
| Boolean | Function |
| Undefined | Date |
| Null | Map |
| Symbol | Set |
| BigInt | Other objects |

> 💡 This concept is especially important in React because **objects and arrays are frequently used as state and props**.

