# 5. Template Literals

Template literals provide a convenient way to create strings and insert variables or expressions.

They use **backticks**:

```javascript
`
`
```

Instead of normal quotes:

```javascript
""
''
```

---

## 🔹 Basic Example

```javascript
const name = "Abhinav";

console.log(`Hello ${name}`);
```

Output:

```text
Hello Abhinav
```

The `${ }` syntax is called **interpolation**.

---

## 🔹 Multiple Variables

```javascript
const name = "Abhinav";
const age = 20;

console.log(`My name is ${name} and I am ${age} years old.`);
```

Output:

```text
My name is Abhinav and I am 20 years old.
```

---

## 🔹 Expressions

You can also use JavaScript expressions inside `${ }`.

```javascript
const a = 10;
const b = 20;

console.log(`Total: ${a + b}`);
```

Output:

```text
Total: 30
```

---

## 🔹 Multiline Strings

Template literals can easily create multiline strings.

```javascript
const message = `
Hello Abhinav,
Welcome to JavaScript.
Keep learning React!
`;

console.log(message);
```

